 "kind": "statement",
 "title": "Deligne–Laumon affine-line estimate",
 "statement": "Let $j:U_0\\hookrightarrow A_0$ be a dense open immersion and let $F$\nbe a lisse Weil sheaf, punctually $\\iota$-pure of weight $w$ and\nunramified at infinity. For every integer $r$,\n\n$$\nH_c^r(A,j_*F)\\quad\\text{has weights at most }w+r.\n$$\n\nMore precisely, degree zero vanishes, degree two is pure of weight\n$w+2$, and only the degree-one upper bound requires the Fourier argument.",
 "source": {
  "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-deligne-laumon-a1/sk-thm-dl-a1-upper/_section.md",
  "place": "node sk-thm-dl-a1-upper, order 3129"
 },
 "stmt_deps": [
  "sk-d-dl-a1-setup"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "The endpoint lemma settles all degrees except one. Choose a finite\ncomposition filtration of the Weil representation of $F$; finite\ndimension ensures termination. Its lisse irreducible factors remain\npure of weight $w$, since their stalk spectra are subquotient spectra,\nand remain unramified at infinity.",
   "uses": [],
   "elementary": null
  },
  {
   "text": "A geometrically constant factor extends constantly to $A_0$ and has\n$H_c^1=0$, since $R\\Gamma_c(A,\\DLAQ)=\\DLAQ(-1)[-2]$.\nEvery other factor satisfies [sk-c-dl-a1-hc1]. By\n[sk-p-dl-a1-exact], each filtration step remains a short exact\nsequence after applying $j_*$. The compact-support long exact sequence\nthen gives an exact triple in degree one. Its middle term inherits\nthe common upper bound from its two outer terms, because characteristic\npolynomials multiply in short exact sequences of Frobenius modules.\nInduction through the filtration proves the result. No splitting of\nthe filtration or semisimplicity theorem for pure sheaves is required.",
   "uses": [],
   "elementary": null
  }
 ],
 "proof_deps": [
